## Why the two gaps exist (and what "ready" actually means)

The automation logic is **fully built**. Every node, every edge, every field mapping is done and the file validates clean. The two open items aren't gaps in the flow design — they're **configuration steps that only you can fill in**, because they require credentials and infrastructure that live on your tenant:

| Step | Why I can't do it for you |
|---|---|
| Deploy IxP model to Orchestrator folder | Requires you to pick a folder on your Orchestrator — I don't know your folder structure or which folder your SAP process should run in |
| Create SAP BAPI connection | Requires your actual SAP hostname, system number, client, username, and password — I don't have those |

Neither of these is development work. They're one-time setup clicks in the UiPath UI. Once they're done, you swap the mock node for the real one in Studio Web (drag and drop), run `node configure` with the sidecar file I left you, and the automation runs without you touching anything.

---

## How it actually sends data to SAP — step by step

Here's exactly what happens when a PDF invoice lands in your folder and the flow runs:

**Step 1 — Extract Invoice Fields**
Your IxP model (the one we trained with F1=1.00 on 10 real invoices) reads the PDF and returns a structured object. Every field you said you needed comes out of this:

```
Invoice Number  →  "INV-2024-0042"
Vendor Name     →  "Acme Supplies Ltd"
Invoice Date    →  "2024-07-15"
Total Amount    →  "4,250.00"
Line Items      →  [ { description, qty, unit price, amount }, … ]
```

**Step 2 — Map Extracted Fields (the JavaScript script node)**
This node takes the raw IxP output and reshapes it into the format SAP expects. Here's what the script actually does — nothing hidden:

```javascript
const fields = ixpOut.ExtractionResult.ResultsDocument.Fields;
const get = (name) => fields.find(f => f.FieldName === name)?.Values?.[0] ?? null;

return {
  invoiceNumber: get('Invoice Number'),   // → SAP Reference field
  vendorName:    get('Vendor Name'),      // → SAP Header Text
  invoiceDate:   get('Invoice Date'),     // → SAP Document Date + Posting Date
  totalAmount:   get('Total Amount'),     // → SAP Amount
  lineItems:     tables                   // → SAP line-item table
};
```

**Step 3 — Post Invoice to SAP**
This node calls SAP through the `uipath-sap-bapi` Integration Service connector, which uses SAP's **RFC/BAPI protocol** (the same underlying mechanism SAP uses for all external system integrations — this is the official, SAP-supported way to post documents). The specific call is:

- **BAPI:** `BAPI_ACC_DOCUMENT_POST` — this is the standard SAP BAPI for posting accounting documents (vendor invoices, AP entries). It's what SAP themselves recommend for automated invoice posting.
- **What it does in SAP:** Creates a vendor invoice document in Financial Accounting (FI), the same document that would appear if you'd typed it in manually via transaction FB60 or MIRO.
- **Data it sends:** Company code, document date, posting date, vendor account, amount, currency, reference (your invoice number), header text (vendor name)

The connector handles the RFC connection, authentication, and the COMMIT BAPI call automatically — you don't write any SAP-specific code.

**Error handling:** If the SAP call fails (wrong BAPI parameters, connection down, SAP returns an error), the flow routes to the **Terminate on Error** node instead of silently swallowing the failure. You'd see the failed instance in Orchestrator with the full error message from SAP.

---

## Bottom line

The flow does exactly what you described doing by hand — read the PDF, pull out the fields, post to SAP. The two configuration steps are the equivalent of "give it your SAP password" and "tell it which folder to watch" — there's no way to pre-fill those without your actual credentials. Once those are done (15–20 minutes in the UI), this runs automatically every time a PDF arrives.

